A||||19||grinthock||Water Based Air condition for your home||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||07-19-2001||08:16 PM||||OK stupid question here<P>The building I live in has VERY cold water pretty much 24/7 even when it's hot out, is there a way I could use that to cool my APT?  The landlord pays for the water||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||07-19-2001||08:55 PM||||Get a radiator, run the water through it, put a fan in front of it to draw the air through it.<BR>My friends dad did that since they have an artisian well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||07-19-2001||08:57 PM||||Put a sprinkler in the dining room.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||AusFiero||07-19-2001||09:18 PM||||You can cool outside area with it well Grinthock. Construct a 4 sided box on the roof of any under cover area. the sides should be louvered. Insert plain old thick corrugated cardboard in the sides with lots of holes punched into them and a drainage sytem at the bottom of each area. Basically you run a hose to the area that allows the cardboard to become soaked, when soaked it allows air to flow through your cooling tower and all the cold air drops.<P>Can go high tech with sensors and stuff with it too to make it always stay wet, or just turn the hose on to wet it again whenever the air below it starts to cool.<P>Have seen large scale versions of this working in huge outdoor malls. Works real well. Was a little project of mine while studying Architecture. The I found someone had already done it before. So that blew that instant millions idea  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroBert||07-19-2001||11:54 PM||||Come on out to Phoenix and get an Evaporative Cooler (Swamp Cooler).  Standard item on a lot of homes out here.  The draw back is that they add humidity to your air inside the house.  They work until well into the 90 degree range though if the outside air humidity is relatively low.  Similar to what AusFiero describes, most are roof mounted units with louvered sides with water soaked pads on the sides.  Blower inside sucks air thru the pads and blows into existing air ducts in the house.  Need to keep a window or door open to increase the air flow or install special vents in the ceiling to allow the air to escape.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||07-20-2001||01:40 AM||||That's why you get a piggyback unit.<P>Besides, how "hot" can it get? Its Canada.||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||07-20-2001||02:43 AM||||They make some matted material to take the place of louvers or the cardboard mentioned above. They use a small area in the bottom as a tank fitted with a float valve to control the water. Water is piped in via plastic or copper tubing like the icemaker in modern refridgerators. A large swamp coler sells for about $600 on sale in the fall at most Lowes. For more info,go to ctdoc.com. There are about a dozen "lessons" there at the bottom of the home page. Lesson 1 is about 'latent heat vaporization', which is how evap coolers work. Also you may want to read about treating the water for mineral deposits. Legionaire's disease is also a remote possibility with these things. We are building a large one at work, but our cooling tower is in the shade too much of the time. Ya have to have dry warm air to make the moisture evaporate, giving up heat in the process.<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||maryjane||07-20-2001||02:53 AM||||Fierobert, why do the swamp coolers not work in 90 deg or above temp?<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000008||grinthock||07-20-2001||06:18 AM||||swap coolers wo'nt work because they are basically a heat exchanger / heat pump... oh and for your information it hits 120 deg F here in Canada once or twice during the summer and 100-105 is a pretty normal summer day.....<P>The problem I have here is i live in a ground level Apartment....||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wolfhound||07-20-2001||07:20 AM||||I just worked on a house that has an under ground cooling system. Pipe was buried in a grid in the front yard. Water is pumped through as a  heat exchange. Underground temp is a constant 52 Degrees. It works well.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||07-20-2001||09:53 AM||||anything you try to rig up to extract the heat from your apt with the cold water from the tap will cost more to operate than a simple window air conditioner.<P>  just because your landlord pays for the water, and you pay for the elect now, doesnt mean if you start using $200 on month worth the water that he is gonna smile and go along with it.<P>  most likely the landlord will throw your a$$ out in the street if you start 'wasting water' and the courts would be on his side.<P>  if you are short on funds best way to stay cool is to get a small window A/C and only use it for one room - most likely the bedroom, and keep the door closed and the shades pulled to keep the hot sunlight out.<P>  and look forward to the day when you have your own house and its your wife and kids that stay home all day, running the central AC at 72°F - and you get to pay the $200/month elect bill.  :c)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||07-20-2001||09:56 AM||||well ok - there is one way<P> fill the tub up with cold tap water.<P> and sit in it for a hour, up to your ears.<P> your body core temp will drop a few degrees and you'll feel better.||||reg||1
Z||000012||JSocha||07-20-2001||09:57 AM||||The guy I bought my house off of last November was a trench digger/contractor before he sold his business out to MAZTEC.  While building his mansion (monument to himself), he installed a heat pump system in his house and buried, get this, <B>2 miles of tube for the system 24' in the ground!</B>  Only 18% of that is being used and it has three manifolds in my basement.  I can cool my house down to 42<sup>o</sup> (no problems) and did just to check out his claim here on Monday when he sold me the house.  We got it down to 38<sup>o</sup> before my wife and kids told me to quit it and turn on the heat.  Only draw back was that my utility room in my basement was now raining from the condensation on the pipes and the floor heat tubes stapled to the joists.<P>As I kid you not and if anybody here decides to visit ND, I encourage them to stop by and visit us and I will show, if not, prove it to them, especially in 90+ weather and high humidity.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnnyK||07-20-2001||04:36 PM||||Well, it easily gets up into the 90's here, and considering he is only about 45 minutes away from me, I'd say the climate changed are kept to a minimum..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||07-20-2001||04:51 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>swap coolers wo'nt work because they are basically a heat exchanger / heat pump... oh and for your information it hits 120 deg F here in Canada once or twice during the summer and 100-105 is a pretty normal summer day.....<P>The problem I have here is i live in a ground level Apartment....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> I think your °C to °F conversion equation is a little off - people start to die around 110 F.<P> EDIT: by any chance is your outside thermometer out in the direct sunlight?<P><p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroHeather||07-20-2001||08:30 PM||||It gets to be around 80 to 100F on a normal summer day.  It has hit 110 a couple of times this summer, followed by a rainy period afterwords.<P>My favourite seasons are the times when the temperatures outside are about the same as they are inside--maybe even just a tad cooler outside  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||grinthock||07-20-2001||09:05 PM||||Nope, it DOES get to 120 here... Maybe one day a year, but it does.... No Friggen IGLOOS HERE!||||reg||1
Z||000017||Songman||07-21-2001||04:22 PM||||There was a great article about something like this in Popular Mechanics a few years ago.. They ran tubes filled with water underground all around the house... I forget how many thousands of gallons of water... and it was circulated through a pump in the house. I am not exactly sure how it worked but it somehow took heat out of your house to cool, and took cold out to heat. Neat concept but looked very expensive to build.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Mach10||07-21-2001||04:47 PM||||It's based on the same principle on the Air exchanger in my house, but on a much larger scale. The unit operates on temperature differential. A highly thermal-conductive material separates the incoming and outgoing airstreams (looks like corrugated cardboard) and uses the temperature differential to control the incoming air temps.<BR>EG., if it's 21*C outside, and 17*C inside, the incoming air is cooled by the exhaust air, which lowers the incoming to about 18*C. In -20*C weather, the incoming air is warmed up to 18*C by the 20*C interior air.<BR>This doesn't in itself do too much. HOWEVER, at the same time, the air coming in and being cooled has it's moisture stripped out of it by the exchanger, thus lowering the percieved temperature even more. ||||reg||7
Z||000019||JSocha||07-23-2001||09:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>There was a great article about something like this in Popular Mechanics a few years ago.. They ran tubes filled with water underground all around the house... I forget how many thousands of gallons of water... and it was circulated through a pump in the house. I am not exactly sure how it worked but it somehow took heat out of your house to cool, and took cold out to heat. Neat concept but looked very expensive to build.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ground Source Heat Pump.<BR>======================================<BR>That's what I have in my house to heat and cool with.  In a normal installation, the ground will heat/cool it down to around 60 degrees (or whatever the ground temperature it is at the depth it was burried at/to).  In my case down under the 40 degree mark because it was trenched in so deep.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
